⚡ Java Collection Deep Dive — PriorityQueue (Internal Working)
1. Introduction

PriorityQueue is a Queue implementation that orders its elements according to their natural order or a custom Comparator.
Unlike normal queues (FIFO), it removes elements based on priority, not insertion order.

✅ Default = Min-Heap (smallest element has highest priority)
❌ Not thread-safe
✅ Allows null → ❌ No (throws NullPointerException)

2. Internal Data Structure

Internally, PriorityQueue uses a binary heap, represented as an array (not a tree object).
Each element’s position satisfies the heap property:

For Min-Heap: parent <= children

For Max-Heap: parent >= children (if Comparator provided)

3. Source Code Snapshot (Simplified)
public class PriorityQueue<E> extends AbstractQueue<E>
    implements java.io.Serializable {

    private transient Object[] queue;  // array-based heap
    private int size = 0;
    private final Comparator<? super E> comparator;

    public boolean offer(E e) {
        if (e == null) throw new NullPointerException();
        int i = size;
        if (i >= queue.length) grow();
        siftUp(i, e); // restore heap property
        size = i + 1;
        return true;
    }

    public E poll() {
        if (size == 0) return null;
        int s = --size;
        E result = (E) queue[0];
        E x = (E) queue[s];
        queue[s] = null;
        if (s != 0)
            siftDown(0, x); // restore heap property
        return result;
    }
}

4. Internal Working
Adding an element (offer/add)

Element inserted at the end of array.

Then it’s “sifted up” to its correct position to maintain heap order.

Compares with parent → swaps if smaller (in Min-Heap).

Removing an element (poll)

Removes the root element (highest priority).

Moves the last element to root.

Performs “sift down” to restore heap order.

5. Heap Index Calculations

For any index i:

Parent(i) = (i - 1) / 2
Left(i) = 2 * i + 1
Right(i) = 2 * i + 2

