7. Example Code
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

public class PQDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();
        pq.add(30);
        pq.add(20);
        pq.add(50);
        pq.add(10);

        System.out.println(pq); // not in sorted order
        System.out.println(pq.poll()); // 10
        System.out.println(pq.poll()); // 20
    }
}


Output:

[10, 20, 50, 30]
10
20


(Internal array order ≠ sorted order — only heap property maintained)

8. Custom Comparator Example
PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> b - a);
maxHeap.add(10);
maxHeap.add(30);
maxHeap.add(20);
System.out.println(maxHeap.poll()); // 30


✅ Acts as Max-Heap when comparator reverses order.

9. Time Complexity
Operation	Time Complexity
add / offer	O(log n)
remove / poll	O(log n)
peek	O(1)
iteration	O(n)
10. Null Handling

Does not allow nulls

add(null) → NullPointerException

11. Important Points

✅ Implemented using array-based binary heap
✅ No nulls or heterogeneous types
✅ Not thread-safe
✅ Can use custom comparator for max/min behavior
✅ Doesn’t guarantee order during iteration
✅ Used heavily in Dijkstra’s, A*, Job scheduling, etc.

